# The Star Weaver's Gift

In the time before time, when the sky was empty and black, there lived a weaver named Celeste who dwelt alone on a mountain peak. She possessed a loom made of moonbeams and threads spun from light itself.

Each night, Celeste worked tirelessly, weaving patterns into the darkness. She created shimmering tapestries that she would hang across the void, but by morning, they always faded away. This filled her heart with sorrow, for she longed to create something lasting.

One evening, a small child climbed the mountain and asked, "Why do you weave what disappears?"

Celeste replied, "I weave beauty, even if it cannot endure."

The child smiled and said, "Perhaps your gift is not meant to last forever, but to return each night, giving hope to those below."

Understanding bloomed in Celeste's heart. She took her finest threads and wove them into countless tiny points of light, scattering them across the heavens. These became the stars—eternal yet ever-changing, forming new patterns as they wheeled through the seasons.

From that night forward, the stars have remained, reminding all who gaze upward that even fleeting beauty, when shared generously, can become immortal.
